1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,501
Hi everyone,

2
00:00:01,521 --> 00:00:07,026
and welcome back to Sewing Machine Design and Manufacturing at WenQuest.

3
00:00:07,426 --> 00:00:11,802
This is Lecture 5, the hook mechanism.

4
00:00:12,202 --> 00:00:16,988
The part spinning fast in one direction under the bed plate is the full

5
00:00:17,008 --> 00:00:20,508
rotary hook that industrial lockstitch machines use.

6
00:00:20,528 --> 00:00:25,924
On every stitch its point has a split second to catch the loop of needle thread.

7
00:00:25,944 --> 00:00:31,000
A little early or late, too far or too close, and you get a skipped stitch,

8
00:00:31,020 --> 00:00:33,302
a split thread or a needle strike.

9
00:00:34,822 --> 00:00:36,551
Here's the problem.

10
00:00:36,951 --> 00:00:42,528
A garment factory switches a shirt line to jeans.

11
00:00:42,928 --> 00:00:51,468
The mechanic changes the needle from Nm 90 to Nm 110 and leaves the hook alone.

12
00:00:51,868 --> 00:00:59,015
The clearance was set at 0.06 millimetres and the timing at 2.0 millimetres

13
00:00:59,035 --> 00:01:00,448
of needle rise.

14
00:01:00,848 --> 00:01:05,944
Now the hook clatters, and after a few hundred stitches the needle snaps.

15
00:01:06,344 --> 00:01:11,852
The supervisor also wants the timing at 2.5 millimetres for denim,

16
00:01:11,872 --> 00:01:13,779
as the manual suggests.

17
00:01:14,179 --> 00:01:17,920
What needs adjusting, and by how much?

18
00:01:19,440 --> 00:01:21,100
What the hook has to do.

19
00:01:22,620 --> 00:01:24,840
What does the hook do in one stitch?

20
00:01:25,240 --> 00:01:28,540
Watch the prototype at one-hundredth speed.

21
00:01:28,940 --> 00:01:34,327
First, the catch: the needle rises, a loop bulges out by the scarf,

22
00:01:34,347 --> 00:01:38,120
and the point slips in between needle and loop.

23
00:01:38,520 --> 00:01:44,719
Then it draws the loop larger and carries it round the bobbin case,

24
00:01:44,739 --> 00:01:48,060
so the bobbin thread passes through.

25
00:01:48,460 --> 00:01:53,789
Finally cast-off: the loop slips off the case and the lever pulls it tight.

26
00:01:54,189 --> 00:01:59,008
The later steps depend on the hook's shape and rarely need adjusting;

27
00:01:59,028 --> 00:02:02,936
the catch is extremely sensitive to position and timing,

28
00:02:02,956 --> 00:02:04,689
and that's today's focus.

29
00:02:06,209 --> 00:02:10,782
Why two turns of the hook for one of the shaft?

30
00:02:11,182 --> 00:02:18,383
From catching the loop to casting it off, the hook turns about 270 degrees.

31
00:02:18,783 --> 00:02:25,280
At two to one that takes only 135 degrees of shaft: catch at 206,

32
00:02:25,300 --> 00:02:26,884
cast-off at 341,

33
00:02:26,904 --> 00:02:33,402
more than a hundred degrees before the next needle enters at 102,

34
00:02:33,422 --> 00:02:38,415
time for the lever to tighten and the dog to feed.

35
00:02:38,815 --> 00:02:46,721
At one to one the loop wouldn't come off until 116 degrees into the next stitch,

36
00:02:46,741 --> 00:02:51,279
but the needle is already in the cloth at 102.

37
00:02:51,679 --> 00:02:54,779
The hook spends one idle turn to buy that time.

38
00:02:56,299 --> 00:03:02,159
Two to one has a direct consequence: the hook turns very fast.

39
00:03:02,559 --> 00:03:11,459
At 5000 r/min on the shaft the hook does 10000.

40
00:03:11,859 --> 00:03:18,255
Take the point radius as 13.5 millimetres, our example value,

41
00:03:18,275 --> 00:03:22,567
and it moves at about 14 metres a second.

42
00:03:22,967 --> 00:03:27,306
Picture it: a steel point doing 14 metres a second,

43
00:03:27,326 --> 00:03:33,546
brushing past a needle less than a millimetre thick with a gap of under a

44
00:03:33,566 --> 00:03:35,426
tenth of a millimetre.

45
00:03:35,826 --> 00:03:40,526
That's why the catch is so sensitive to position and timing.

46
00:03:42,046 --> 00:03:44,586
Three things you can adjust.

47
00:03:46,106 --> 00:03:51,278
On a finished machine there are only three things you can adjust in the field.

48
00:03:51,678 --> 00:03:54,969
Look at the needle from the scarf side:

49
00:03:54,989 --> 00:03:59,978
the loop bulges out there and the hook point sweeps across.

50
00:04:00,378 --> 00:04:01,538
One, timing:

51
00:04:01,558 --> 00:04:09,109
expressed as how far the needle has risen from its lowest point when the hook

52
00:04:09,129 --> 00:04:12,648
point reaches the needle centreline,

53
00:04:12,668 --> 00:04:13,533
the rise.

54
00:04:13,933 --> 00:04:15,853
Two, needle-bar height:

55
00:04:15,873 --> 00:04:22,346
at the catch the point should be about a millimetre above the top of the eye.

56
00:04:22,746 --> 00:04:28,478
Three, the clearance between point and needle surface;

57
00:04:28,498 --> 00:04:33,166
one manual asks for 0.04 to 0.1 millimetres.

58
00:04:34,686 --> 00:04:37,546
Why rise rather than angle?

59
00:04:37,946 --> 00:04:41,881
Near the bottom the needle curve is flat,

60
00:04:41,901 --> 00:04:49,406
and the rise goes with the square of the angle past bottom dead centre, delta.

61
00:04:49,806 --> 00:04:59,146
Two millimetres of rise means delta of about 26 degrees, a catch at 206.1.

62
00:04:59,546 --> 00:05:01,800
But mind the sensitivity:

63
00:05:01,820 --> 00:05:08,169
five degrees either way and the rise goes from 1.3 to 2.8 millimetres,

64
00:05:08,189 --> 00:05:11,535
across the whole range in the manual.

65
00:05:11,935 --> 00:05:16,915
To adjust, loosen the screws and turn the hook; at two to one,

66
00:05:16,935 --> 00:05:20,785
a degree of hook is only half a degree of shaft.

67
00:05:22,305 --> 00:05:24,347
The second is needle-bar height.

68
00:05:24,747 --> 00:05:25,829
At the catch,

69
00:05:25,849 --> 00:05:32,350
the lower edge of the hook point should be a little above the top of the eye,

70
00:05:32,370 --> 00:05:38,872
so the point enters the loop above the eye without catching the thread in it.

71
00:05:39,272 --> 00:05:42,877
The manual says 1.0 millimetre above.

72
00:05:43,277 --> 00:05:49,627
To adjust, loosen the needle-bar connector from Lecture 3 and slide the bar.

73
00:05:50,027 --> 00:05:55,416
It changes only the eye's absolute height, not how the bar moves.

74
00:05:55,816 --> 00:06:02,516
Some manuals simply put two marks on the bar, which is very handy in the field.

75
00:06:04,036 --> 00:06:05,816
The third is clearance.

76
00:06:06,216 --> 00:06:11,273
This is a section at the height of the point, seen from above:

77
00:06:11,293 --> 00:06:15,777
the circle is the needle, and the point sweeps past it.

78
00:06:16,177 --> 00:06:21,954
You set it by sliding the hook along its shaft;

79
00:06:21,974 --> 00:06:27,997
a typical requirement is 0.04 to 0.1 millimetres.

80
00:06:28,397 --> 00:06:33,456
The hook also has a needle guard resting lightly against the needle,

81
00:06:33,476 --> 00:06:38,459
so when thick cloth pushes the needle aside it can't hit the point.

82
00:06:38,859 --> 00:06:42,777
Remember: the clearance is set for one needle size.

83
00:06:43,177 --> 00:06:45,315
The next slide shows why.

84
00:06:46,835 --> 00:06:48,935
This is a shop-floor rule.

85
00:06:49,335 --> 00:06:56,081
The Nm number divided by 100 is roughly the needle diameter in millimetres.

86
00:06:56,481 --> 00:07:02,773
From Nm 90 to Nm 110 the diameter grows by 0.2 millimetres,

87
00:07:02,793 --> 00:07:08,657
so the surface moves half that, 0.10, toward the point:

88
00:07:08,677 --> 00:07:15,611
a clearance of 0.06 becomes -0.04, and the point hits the needle.

89
00:07:16,011 --> 00:07:21,939
Go the other way, to Nm 70, and it becomes 0.16;

90
00:07:21,959 --> 00:07:27,391
the point misses the loop and stitches skip.

91
00:07:27,791 --> 00:07:31,325
So after any needle change, reset the clearance.

92
00:07:32,845 --> 00:07:35,197
Lab 5-1 shows it directly.

93
00:07:35,597 --> 00:07:40,851
On the right, an Nm 90 needle at the standard clearance:

94
00:07:40,871 --> 00:07:43,017
the point passes clear.

95
00:07:43,417 --> 00:07:49,687
On the left, only the needle changed to Nm 110, hook untouched:

96
00:07:49,707 --> 00:07:56,077
the needle surface has grown outward, the clearance is negative,

97
00:07:56,097 --> 00:07:59,073
and the point hits the needle.

98
00:07:59,472 --> 00:08:02,812
That's the clatter on the shop floor.

99
00:08:03,212 --> 00:08:08,264
When you hear it, stop at once and check the point and the needle;

100
00:08:08,284 --> 00:08:11,261
stone off any burr or replace the part.

101
00:08:12,781 --> 00:08:13,641
The catch window.

102
00:08:15,161 --> 00:08:17,341
Why must the three be set together?

103
00:08:17,741 --> 00:08:22,347
We need to know how big the loop is at the catch and where.

104
00:08:22,747 --> 00:08:30,049
A real loop depends on thread stiffness, twist and how the cloth grips it,

105
00:08:30,069 --> 00:08:35,887
and is hard to compute, so the book uses a schematic model.

106
00:08:36,287 --> 00:08:41,731
As the needle rises the loop bulges out by the scarf,

107
00:08:41,751 --> 00:08:48,947
growing to a maximum near 2.2 millimetres of rise, then closing again.

108
00:08:49,347 --> 00:08:57,247
The point catches it if the bulge exceeds the clearance plus the point's thickness.

109
00:08:57,647 --> 00:09:02,984
Too near the eye splits the thread; zero clearance hits the needle.

110
00:09:04,504 --> 00:09:10,427
Check every point on the plane of hook angle against needle-bar height,

111
00:09:10,447 --> 00:09:15,282
and the points that catch form a region: the catch window,

112
00:09:15,302 --> 00:09:18,044
darker green meaning more margin.

113
00:09:18,444 --> 00:09:21,744
Right, woven cloth; left, knit.

114
00:09:22,144 --> 00:09:26,176
Knits stretch, the loop is small and unsteady,

115
00:09:26,196 --> 00:09:31,284
and the window is clearly narrower; that's why knits skip.

116
00:09:31,684 --> 00:09:37,840
On the shop floor: first fit a ball-point needle and check how it's fitted,

117
00:09:37,860 --> 00:09:41,381
then take the clearance to its lower limit;

118
00:09:41,401 --> 00:09:44,922
don't just keep moving the bar up and down.

119
00:09:46,442 --> 00:09:50,741
On screen the machine is scrambled, as if badly set up,

120
00:09:50,761 --> 00:09:54,354
and the working point jumps out of the window;

121
00:09:54,374 --> 00:10:00,636
then it's restored to the manual's standard and the point is back in the middle.

122
00:10:01,036 --> 00:10:03,419
Note the slant: catch later,

123
00:10:03,439 --> 00:10:07,883
and by the time the point arrives the eye has risen,

124
00:10:07,903 --> 00:10:13,376
so the point is relatively low; to compensate you lower the bar.

125
00:10:13,776 --> 00:10:19,689
That's why the manual says timing first, then height, then clearance,

126
00:10:19,709 --> 00:10:24,504
and why its standard point sits mid-window, with margin.

127
00:10:26,024 --> 00:10:28,723
After the catch, and field notes.

128
00:10:30,243 --> 00:10:35,476
Catching the loop and a robot picking moving parts off a conveyor are the

129
00:10:35,496 --> 00:10:36,987
same kind of problem:

130
00:10:37,007 --> 00:10:40,873
the tool must reach a moving target at the right time,

131
00:10:40,893 --> 00:10:43,823
the right height and the right clearance.

132
00:10:44,223 --> 00:10:48,508
The sewing machine locks its hook to the shaft phase mechanically;

133
00:10:48,528 --> 00:10:53,465
the robot uses the conveyor encoder as its shaft and corrects the pick point

134
00:10:53,485 --> 00:10:54,312
continuously,

135
00:10:54,332 --> 00:10:56,073
which is conveyor tracking.

136
00:10:56,473 --> 00:11:01,003
A thicker needle means resetting the clearance;

137
00:11:01,023 --> 00:11:06,423
a new gripper means recalibrating the tool centre point,

138
00:11:06,443 --> 00:11:09,133
or it crashes into the part.

139
00:11:10,653 --> 00:11:14,953
An everyday example: the baton change in a relay race,

140
00:11:14,973 --> 00:11:16,953
which has its own window.

141
00:11:17,353 --> 00:11:22,307
If the receiver starts too early, the runner can't catch up;

142
00:11:22,327 --> 00:11:24,463
too late and they collide;

143
00:11:24,483 --> 00:11:28,940
hold the hand at the wrong height and the baton drops.

144
00:11:29,340 --> 00:11:35,121
Timing, height and distance have to work together, exactly like the hook:

145
00:11:35,141 --> 00:11:41,080
catch timing, needle-bar height and clearance, and none of them can be off.

146
00:11:42,600 --> 00:11:44,577
Back to the start.

147
00:11:44,977 --> 00:11:52,080
The thicker needle's surface moves toward the point by half the diameter difference,

148
00:11:52,100 --> 00:11:58,525
0.10 millimetres, so the clearance becomes -0.04: the point hits the needle,

149
00:11:58,545 --> 00:12:01,917
hence the clatter and the broken needle.

150
00:12:02,317 --> 00:12:06,200
Slide the hook out 0.10 millimetres.

151
00:12:06,600 --> 00:12:13,915
By the approximate formula 2.0 millimetres of rise is at 205.7 degrees and

152
00:12:13,935 --> 00:12:15,204
2.5 at 208.7,

153
00:12:15,224 --> 00:12:20,656
about 3 degrees of shaft; the hook turns twice as fast,

154
00:12:20,676 --> 00:12:24,620
so turn it about 6 degrees on its shaft.

155
00:12:25,020 --> 00:12:28,760
Then check the bar height, and set the clearance last.

156
00:12:30,280 --> 00:12:35,759
Now Virtual lab 5-1. On the WenQuest platform you can do it yourself.

157
00:12:35,779 --> 00:12:41,656
